        "content": "Let me analyze this step by step using chain of thought reasoning.\n\nFirst, I need to understand what the images show and what the clinical context tells us:\n\n**Clinical history**: Strong coughing attacks (suggests increased intrathoracic pressure)\n**Physical exam findings**: Subcutaneous emphysema (air in subcutaneous tissues)\n\n**Image analysis**:\n- Figure 1a (PA view): Shows air in the soft tissues of the neck (yellow arrows) - this is subcutaneous emphysema. The red arrows point to air in the mediastinum along the heart border and around the aorta.\n- Figure 1b (lateral view): Shows air in the mediastinum (red arrows) - particularly along the anterior and posterior mediastinal borders.\n\nThe key finding here is **air in the mediastinum** (pneumomediastinum) with associated subcutaneous emphysema, but no obvious pneumothorax (no visible visceral pleural line with lung collapse).\n\nGiven the clinical history of strong coughing attacks, this strongly suggests **spontaneous pneumomediastinum** - where increased intrathoracic pressure from forceful coughing causes alveolar rupture, allowing air to track along bronchovascular bundles into the mediastinum, and then potentially into the subcutaneous tissues.\n\nLet me verify this interpretation with the tools:\n\n",
